/*
 * MapKnower - mmcg@bruce.cs.monash.edu.au, 1991 (Gandalf @ Shattered World).
 *
 * Updated (test) version, for world code: can't sensibly treat
 * the world as a single area.
 *
 * The world is a 3-D coord system, with collections of (old-style)
 * areas embedded within it.  We have a world server, from which
 * we can obtain lists of entrances to these areas (though not the
 * area names), and blockages in the world (usually to wall off
 * an area).  Areas under the old map system (still supported)
 * always store a single exit to each adjacent area - simple, but
 * surprisingly functional - and the route calculated was the
 * one that traversed the minimal no. of areas.  With the outside world
 * now representing a single area, this won't work so well - by
 * an accident of fate (when the map was being built), a route
 * within a city to a spot 3 rooms away could involve walking
 * out the gates, around the walls, backj in the other side.
 *
 * BUT: the cool thing about having a MapKnower is that it, uhh,
 * knows about the map.  In particular, it can work out which
 * areas (eg collections of rooms) are directly connected to
 * which other areas.  Since `areas' here are simply a device to
 * allow route calculation through arbitrary graphs, and `real' areas
 * (like Sanom, or Merenth) are no longer directly connected,
 * but are instead embedded in (quite distant) parts of the world,
 * we now have a way of figuring out all of the rooms within Sanom (or
 * Merenth) - and hence of working out all of the entrances to
 * a given city.  This gives us a hell of a lot more than routes :)
 *
 * SO: we list the old concepts and define some new ones:
 *	1. A room is an old-style room, with a dest-dir.  Each is in an area.
 *	2. An area is an old-style area, containing only old-style rooms.
 *	   As before, each area lists ONE exit to each adjacent area.
 *	   Areas are defined by this object (as part of auto-mapping).
 *	   Each area is in a cluster.
 *	3. A CONTROLLER is an area controller, as before.  Controllers
 *	   behave like areas, but have virtual rooms and their own
 *	   mapping algorithms.  Each controller knows _all_ of its
 *	   exits to adjacent areas and controllers (including the exit
 *	   and entrance rooms, as before).
 *	4. A CLUSTER is a collection of directly-connected areas (that
 *	   don't traverse any rooms managed by a controller).  We merge
 *	   areas into clusters incrementally - each new area is initially
 *	   placed in its own cluster, and whenever a join is found
 *	   between two areas, the clusters are merged (the algorithm is
 *	   incremental and O(1) amortised).  We keep an array of integers
 *	   (one per area) listing which cluster it is currently mapped to.
 *	   All area controllers get a cluster ID of (-1).
 *	5. Controllers (or rooms they control) never belong to clusters.
 *
 **** ORIGINAL COMMENTS
 *
 * Server allowing routes to be quickly computed between rooms.
 * Will also return a list of rooms within some distance of a room,
 * or along a particular direction from a room (jan 95 - G).
 * This server should be called for general room->room movements.
 * It deals with individual rooms, but understands the existence
 * of area controllers, and refers mapping tasks through an area
 * to the individual controllers (it does *not* want to have to keep
 * arrays containing a billion rooms in a virtual sea, and so will not
 * save them).
 *
 * See std/map/track.c for mapper usage.
 *
 * Call this_object()->save_me() to dump the map to a savefile (when you are
 * setting this up, use massacre.c to build the entire map, then call save();
 * the .o file is loaded whenever this file is).
 *
 * Assigns rough x,y coords using standard dest_dirs of connecting rooms.
 * Other dirns (to rooms) are placed in different areas.
 * Areas are effectively rooms with dest_dirs to other areas.
 *
 * Hack 1: Handles SW's standard doors - rooms beyond doors have directions
 * of dir#0code.  When returning routes, directions may have codes - eg west#0iacd
 * 0, 1, 2 are for door states.
 *
 * Hack 1a: rooms which change dest dirs (directions but not destinations) get
 * a direction of 'dir#3:objname'.  Routing objects in the room should try to find
 * the appropriate exit (or give up if it's a player - it's probably a maze :).
 *
 * Hack 2: Room box controllers are treated as a single area; the controller
 * is responsible for computing routes between entrances and exits.  If
 * a room name is given as "a/b", and 'a' exists as a known room box controller,
 * all room will be placed in the same area, and the given controller will be
 * called to compute routes between its rooms (it is supplied with the full path
 * name when this happens).  New controllers are added to the mapknower in two
 * ways: the controller should call MAPKNOWER->add_controller(file_name(this_object()))
 * whenever loaded, and all controlled rooms should call add_location with the controller
 * as the third argument.
 *
 * NOTE: We NEED some standard hooks in rooms to manage this stuff properly.
 *
 * Some code is ripped from my postscript mapper monster.
 *
 * ASSUMPTIONS:
 *	Controlled rooms with exits to other areas do not change their
 *	external exits, and are nosave.
 *
 * BUGS: Num rooms is limited by the size of your arrays (we dont use arrays
 * of arrays here, it gets a little excessive :).
 *	 You have to be sure that cloned (and destructed) rooms don't
 * 	 keep adding themselves (too expensive - will fill array with name#uniquenum).
 *	 Ideally, all such rooms will be virtual objects with known names.
 *	 Doesn't handle updates in room dest dirs - not that anyone uses them!
 *	 Doesn't understand rooms where different players may have different exits.
 *	 Doesn't understand virtual rooms whose controllers aren't a contraction
 *	 of the room name (that's ok - neither does the driver :)
 *	 Assumes that X,Y changes are ok, but Z changes always get new areas.
 *
 * WARNING: uses hash_value in savefiles, if hash_value (efun) changes the
 * savefile will have to be rebuilt.
 */

#include <Servers.h>

#define	ROOM_TABLE_SIZE 4903	
    /* max number of rooms; should be a fairly large prime. */
#define AREA_TABLE_SIZE	1000	
    /* needn't be prime.  Max areas. */
#define MAX_AREA_EFFECT	100	
    /* max num rooms that can have an area effect applied.  Should be cached */

/* We keep caches of the last N inter- and intra- area searches - this helps
 * a lot when many monsters are trying to get to the same place.  You should
 * tune this based on the numbers of monsters trying to use this mapper.
 * NB: actual array is twice this size, plus an initial status array.
 * INTER_AREA: there are a few areas between which paths are required 95% of the time.
 */

#define	INTER_AREA_CACHE 100

/* these are usually bigger, but it's well worth saving them - they are
 * more costly to compute, and there are more of them.
 */

#define	INTRA_AREA_CACHE 250

array inter_cache;
array intra_cache;

#define DEBUG
/* debug */
#ifdef DEBUG
#define	TG(s)	{ catch { tell_object(find_player("gandalf"), (s)+"\n"); } ; }
#else
#define TG(s)
#endif

nosave array std_dirs;		/* list of n,s,e,w */

array cluster_list;		/* 1 entry per area, listing the ID of the lowest area known to be (weakly) connected to this one */
array scc_list;			/* SCCs */
array seen_list;		/* rooms that have been seen, hashed on name */
array dd_list;			/* the array of values assoc'd with rooms */
array area_list;		/* area dest_dirs.  0 if no connections out yet */
array reverse_area_list;
array controller_list;		/* known controllers and their area numbers */
array bad_rooms;		/* rooms which changed dest dir (for the worse) since notifying us */
array bad_areas;		/* areas containing bad rooms (marked as high cost by routing algs) */
int next_area;			/* first entry in area_list is a temp var */
int named_rooms;		/* name has been seen, perhaps in a dest dir */
nosave int loaded_rooms; 	/* rooms actually loaded this reboot */
int known_rooms;		/* rooms whose dest dirs we have */
int new_loc_calls;
int nclusters;
int nsccs;

nosave array room_backtrack;	/* for intra-area heuristic search */
nosave array area_backtrack;	/* array of area numbers for area backtrack */

/*
 * courtesy functions
 */

short() { return "Map Seer"; }

long() { write(short() + ".\n"	+
		"It's a navigation server.\n");
	 show_stats();
}

show_stats() {
    write("Areas: " + next_area
		+ ".\nSccs: " + nsccs
		+ ".\nClusters: " + nclusters
		+ ".\nControllers: " + sizeof(controller_list) / 2
    		+ ".\nNamed rooms: " + named_rooms
		+ ".\nLoaded rooms: " + loaded_rooms
		+ ".\nKnown rooms: " + known_rooms
		+ ".\nBad rooms: " + sizeof(bad_rooms)
		+ ".\nAdd-loc calls: " + new_loc_calls + ".\n");
    write("Controllers: " + implode(controller_list, " ") + "\n");
    write("Caches: inter: " + cache_stats(inter_cache) + "\n" +
    	  "	intra: " + cache_stats(intra_cache) + "\n");
    return 1;
}

reset(arg) {
    if (arg)
	return;

    if (file_size(file_name(this_object()) + ".o") > 0) {
	restore_object(file_name(this_object()));
	}
    else {
        seen_list = allocate(ROOM_TABLE_SIZE);
        dd_list = allocate(ROOM_TABLE_SIZE);
        area_list = allocate(AREA_TABLE_SIZE);
	bad_rooms = ({});
	bad_areas = ({});
    }

    area_backtrack = allocate(AREA_TABLE_SIZE);
    room_backtrack = allocate(ROOM_TABLE_SIZE);

    if (!controller_list)
	controller_list = ({ });

    if (!reverse_area_list)
        reverse_area_list = allocate(AREA_TABLE_SIZE);

    if (!cluster_list)
        cluster_list = allocate(AREA_TABLE_SIZE);

    if (!scc_list)
        scc_list = allocate(AREA_TABLE_SIZE);

    std_dirs = ({ "north",	0,	1,
		  "south",	0,	-1,
		  "east",	1,	0,
		  "west",	-1,	0,
		  "northeast",	1,	1,
		  "southeast",	1,	-1,
		  "northwest",	-1,	1,
		  "southwest",	-1,	-1
		  });

    init_caches();
}

/*
 * Save - not usually called, except after we have rebuilt the
 * initial loadable map and want to make it permanent.
 * Also called (now) when a room gets marked as bad.
 */

set_needs_save() {
    if (!"secure/which_mud"->is_wiz_mud()) {
	    AUTOSAVE_SERV->set_auto_save(this_object());
    }
}

save_me() {
    save_object(file_name(this_object()));
}

/*
 * HASHING FUNCTIONS.
 *
 * These are used to keep track of large arrays of rooms and room dest_dirs.
 * It is better to have the arrays in this object (rather than query the
 * rooms) because we don't want heaps of rooms loaded whenever we try to
 * compute a path.  NOTE: These algorithms dont allow for room deletion.
 */

/*
 * hash a string to an array location
 */

/*
 * Find a name in the array, return its spot (if found) or a place where
 * it could be put (if not found).
 */
hash_name(name) 
{
    int finished;
    int h;
    int ihash = hash_value(name) % sizeof(seen_list);
    int x;

    while (!finished) 
    {
	    h = (ihash + x) % sizeof(seen_list);
	    if (!finished) 
        {
	        if (!(seen_list[h])) return h;

	        if (seen_list[h] == name) return h;
	    }
	    /* else we have to rehash */
	    x += 1;
        if (x >= ROOM_TABLE_SIZE) finished = 1;
    }
/* I wanna know when this happens... */
   throw("Out of hash table space");
}

/*
 * Quick routine to strip the key code off a direction, returning
 * the base direction.
 */

strip_code(s) 
{
    int x;
    x = index(s, '#');
    if (x < 0)
       return s;
    else
       return s[..x-1];
}

/*
 * Given a room name, check whether there is a known controller for it.
 * All such rooms have a controller name which is a contraction
 * of the room name.
 */

controller_name(room) {
    int maxlen = 0;
    int x = 0;
    (int|string) y = 0;
    while (x < sizeof(controller_list)) {
	string c = controller_list[x];
	if (room[..sizeof(c)-1] == c && sizeof(c) > maxlen) {
	    y = c;
	    maxlen = sizeof(c);
	    }
	x += 2;
	}
    return y;
}

/*
 * Given a controller name, if it has an area, return it, otherwise
 * allocate one.
 */

controller_area((object|string) contr) {
    string cname;
    if (objectp(contr))
	cname = file_name(contr);
    else
	cname = contr;
    int x = index(controller_list, cname);
    if (x >= 0)
	return controller_list[x+1];
    else {
	controller_list += ({ cname, next_area });
	area_list[next_area] = ({ });
	reverse_area_list[next_area] = ({ });
	cluster_list[next_area] = (-1);	
	scc_list[next_area] = (-1);	
	nsccs++;
	nclusters++;
	return next_area++;
	}
}

/*
 * Explicitly add a controller.
 * Just uses the above to allocate an area.
 */

add_controller((object|string) contr) {
    return controller_area(contr);
}

/*
 * This hook is called when a room gets initialised with a dest_dir.
 * This should be called with the filename of the room.  If the room
 * is a member of a controlled (room box) area, the room name given
 * should be "controller/roomid", and (preferably) only rooms which
 * connect the controller's area to other areas should be given (the
 * controller could manage a huge area, and will be called to compute
 * routes through itself anyway; we seek to minimise the rooms stored
 * here).  All room names with this format will be added to a single
 * area, and will be ignored if all their exits are internal to
 * that area.  This prevents the mapknower from being overloaded by large
 * virtual areas (the area controllers compute their own routes).
 *
 * This looks for an adjacent room in one of the standard directions.
 * It will place a room in the same area as another iff the directions
 * from a->b and b->a are the reverse (they are both std dirs and their
 * dx, dy are negatives of each other).  Otherwise, the room gets a new area.
 * In some parts of the mud this leads to a lot of areas (e.g. up/d stairwells).
 *
 * If a room has been seen in a dest dir, but never loaded, it gets area (-1)
 * and its info list stores the list of rooms that have mentioned it in its
 * dest dir (unless, of course, the room is in a controlled area, in which
 * case it is always added to that controller's area, though the mapknower may
 * retain a record of it if it connects to any other areas).
 */

add_location(string room, dd, (int|string) controller) 
{
    int h;
    array old_dd; /* if seen but never loaded, this is external refs */
    array entry;
    int x, y;
    int i1, i2;
    int area;
    int changed_dd;


    /* ignore unconnected rooms */
    if (!pointerp(dd)) return 0;	
	
    /* in case they fiddle it on us */
    dd = dd + ({ });		

    /* should have been called by the room itself; be sure it's loaded */
    if (!find_object(room)) return 0;
	
    call_out("try_spec_room", 0, room);
	if (room->query_property("no_map")) return;
    new_loc_calls++;

/*
 * If the room is in a controlled area, we are disinterested in
 * exits to other rooms in the same area, so we strip all references
 * to them from our dest dir (and when _they_ were added, the same will
 * have happened to them).  The area controller itself will manage
 * all intra-area routing.
 */

    if (controller) {
    /* find all exits to different areas */
	area = controller_area(controller);	/* be sure it's assigned */
        old_dd = ({ });
        for (x = 0; x < sizeof(dd); x += 2) {
    /* we really should look in seen list before computing controller name, because hash is fast. */
	    (int|string) c = controller_name(dd[x]);
	    if (c != controller)
	        old_dd += dd[x..x+1];
	    }
    /* if there were no other exits, discard the room; area controller manages it. */
        if (!sizeof(old_dd)) {
	    return 0;
	    }
    /* otherwise, we will be saving this room with the shortened dd */
        dd = old_dd;
        old_dd = 0;
        }

    old_dd = 0;

    h = hash_name(room);

    if (!seen_list[h]) {
/*
 * Room never seen, even in ddir of another loaded room
 * If it's controlled, area has already been assigned (it must
 * have useful exits for us to have gotten here).
 */
/* if it's in bad rooms, but not in seen list, at some stage we gave up on the room totally */
	if (index(bad_rooms, room) >= 0) {
	    return 0;
	    }
	if (!controller) {	/* a room alone... make it an area. */
	    area = next_area++;
	    area_list[area] = ({ });
	    reverse_area_list[area] = ({ });
	    cluster_list[area] = area;
	    scc_list[area] = area;
		nsccs++;
	    nclusters++;
	    }
	seen_list[h] = room;
	dd_list[h] = ({ area, 0, 0, 0, 0 }); /* x, y are 0 */
	named_rooms++;
	loaded_rooms++;
	known_rooms++;
	}
    else { /* room name has been seen before - was it ever fully loaded? */

	/*
	 * Check to see that the room's not changing its exits from reset to reset.
	 * This check is somewhat expensive, and should usually be turned off (by
	 * uncommenting the return after the if statement) after rebuilding the
	 * map several times.
	 */

	if (dd_list[h][0] != (-1)) {	/* in an area already -> processed the first time it was loaded */
	/* return(0); */
	/* Yes - if the dd is different to its previous dd, mark it as bad (or alter it), as follows:
	 * If it simply has additional destinations, add them;
	 * If the destinations are the same but the directions are different, alter the
	 * directions in both the dd list and (if present) in the area list.
	 * If some destinations are missing, if they are to a room in the same area,
	 * mark the room as bad and the area as high cost (I should really split
	 * areas); if not, delete the area link.
	 * If the room isn't already marked as bad, drop out and add new area links;
	 * if it's marked already, they're probably unreliable, so don't re-add them.
	 */
	    array ndd = dd_list[h][4];
	    area = dd_list[h][0];
	    int was_bad = 0;
	    for (x = 0; x < sizeof(dd); x+= 2) {
		int nh = hash_name(dd[x]);
		if ((y = index(ndd, nh)) < 0) {	/* missing room from new dest dir which was in old dd */
		    if ((!seen_list[nh])		/* not there anyway */
		       || (dd_list[nh][0] < 0)) {	/* or not in an area */
			   continue;
			   }
		    }
		else {
		    if (strip_code(dd[x+1]) != strip_code(ndd[y+1])) {
			if (index(ndd[y+1],"#") < 0)
			    ndd[y+1] += "#1:" + dd[x];
			else {
			    if ((i1 = index(ndd[y+1],"1:")) < 0)
			        ndd[y+1] += ";1:" + dd[x];
			    else
				if (ndd[y+1][i1..i1 + sizeof(dd[x])] != "1:"+dd[x]+";") {
				    was_bad++;
				    del_area_link(ndd[y], ndd[y+1]);
				    }
			    }
			}
		    }
		}
	    for (x=0; x < sizeof(ndd); x+=2) {
		if ((y = index(dd, seen_list[ndd[x]])) < 0) {	/* missing room from old dest dir */
		    was_bad++;
		    if ((!seen_list[nh])		/* not there anyway */
		       || (dd_list[nh][0] < 0)) {	/* or not in an area */
			   continue;
			   }
		    }
		}
		if (was_bad) {
		    TG(room + " - bad")
		    if (index(bad_rooms, room) < 0) {
			bad_rooms += ({ room });
			set_needs_save();
			}
		    }
	    return (0);
	    }
	else { /* Room was mentioned in another dd but never added before - we have a list of rooms that have seen it.
		* Each of these rooms must have been loaded and assigned an area at some stage.
		* If it isn't controlled, look at its DD for a standard direction
		* leading to a loaded, uncontrolled room, and set its area, x, y to match (if
		* no such rooms in standard directions, it gets its own area, of course).
		* If it is controlled, we will have deleted all references to/from it to
		* rooms in the same area; we simply need to set up the reversal of the prior
		* references to it.
		*/

	    old_dd = dd_list[h][4]; /* save list of incoming links to room */
				    /* we use this later for area linkage */
	    dd_list[h][4] = 0;	    /* reset outgoing links; we'll save them later */

/* If the room has a controller, we _know_ what area it should be in, and we
 * don't care what its x/y coordinates are (controller does all intra-area
 * mapping, we just provide it with two rooms within its area).  So assign
 * it to the controller's area.
 */
	    if (controller) {
		area = controller_area(controller);
		        dd_list[h] = ({ area,
					0, /* x */
					0, /* y */
				        0, /* used for backtracking */
				        0 });
		}
	    else {
/* No controller.  Look at all rooms in its dest dir; if any are (a) loaded into
 * an area, and (b) have opposing directions for link to it and link back here,
 * and (c) are not themselves controlled (which would mean they have their own area),
 * add the room to that area, giving x/y coords appropriate to the direction.
 */
	        x = (-1);
	        area = (-1);
	        while ((x+=2) < sizeof(dd)) {
		    if (controller_name(dd[x-1])) {
			continue;
			}
		    if (0 > (i1 = index(std_dirs, strip_code(dd[x])))) {
		        continue;
		        }
    /* This exit is in a reasonable direction. */
		    y = hash_name(dd[x-1]);
		    if (seen_list[y] && dd_list[y][0] != (-1)) {
    /* found a loaded, connecting room.  see if it has an exit to us */
		        if (0 > (i2 = index(dd_list[y][4], h))) {
			    continue;
			    }
    /* it does - see if the direction is in std dirs */
		        if (0 > (i2 = index(std_dirs,
					    strip_code(dd_list[y][4][i2 + 1])))) {
			    continue;
			    }
    /* It is - make sure it's the reverse of ours */
		        if ((std_dirs[i1+1] != (-(std_dirs[i2+1])))
		           || (std_dirs[i1+2] != (-(std_dirs[i2+2])))) {
			     continue;
			     }
    /* Good god!  We found a match! */
		        area = dd_list[y][0];
		        dd_list[h] = ({ area,
				        dd_list[y][1] - std_dirs[i1+1],
				        dd_list[y][2] - std_dirs[i1+2],
				        0,
				        0 });
		        break;	/* out of while loop */
		        }	/* if seen_list... */
		    } /* while() thru dest dirs */
		}
/* at this point, if area is positive, we've assigned its dd.  Else it gets its own
 * area.
 */
	    if (area < 0) {
		seen_list[h] = room;
		dd_list[h] = ({ next_area, 0, 0, 0, 0 }); /* x, y are 0 */
		area = next_area++;
		area_list[area] = ({ });
		reverse_area_list[area] = ({ });
		cluster_list[area] = area;
		scc_list[area] = area;
		nsccs++;
		nclusters++;
		}
	    loaded_rooms++;
	    known_rooms++;
	    } /* if (room never loaded) */
	} /* if (room never seen) */

/* Now we have set up the room in an area, and, if no controller, assigned
 * X and Y coordinates.  Run through its dest dir again, looking at all rooms
 * it connects to.  For each room listed, ensure it is in the seen array (if
 * it's a new room, add with area (-1)).  If the room found has area -1, add
 * this room to its entry as mentioning that room, so that from/to queries
 * can work to things just off the map (this is much nicer for the city);
 * Otherwise, add the connection(s) between the two areas.  Then, run through
 * the list of prior references to this room, adding the area back-links from
 * those rooms to this area - this way, all incoming area dest dirs get updated.
 */

/* this never got done in the buggy rooms ... G */

     if (old_dd) { /* other rooms had mentioned us - these are
		    * already in array, add area link from there to here
		    */
	 for (x = 0; x < sizeof(old_dd); x+=2) {
	     i1 = old_dd[x];		/* names in old_dd are already hashed */
	     add_area_link(i1, h, old_dd[x+1]);
	     }
	 }

/* ensure rooms named in our dd are in the array, and, if they are and
 * are in an area, add the area link from here to there.  If they aren't
 * in an area, add a back link from them to us (so that when they are
 * finally loaded, they will be able to check for an area).  Convert all
 * names in dd into hash values.
 */

     for (x = 0; x < sizeof(dd); x += 2) {
	 i1 = hash_name(dd[x]);
	 if (!seen_list[i1]) {						/* add 'em in as seen rooms */
	     seen_list[i1] = dd[x];					/* chuck the name in the seen list */
	     dd_list[i1] = ({ (-1), 0, 0, 0, ({ h, dd[x+1] }) });	/* and start its ref'd area list */
	     named_rooms++;
	     }
	 else
	     if (dd_list[i1][0] < 0) { /* seen but unloaded */
		 if (index(dd_list[i1][4], h) < 0)
		     dd_list[i1][4] = dd_list[i1][4] + ({ h, dd[x+1] });
		 }
	     else	/* seen *and* loaded - add link here->there */
	 	 add_area_link(h, i1, dd[x+1]);
	 dd[x] = i1;
	 }

/* finally, save the transformed dd into our entry */
    dd_list[h][4] = dd;
}

try_spec_room(string room) {
    catch SPEC_ROOMS->loaded_room(room);;
    }

/*
 * Delete a link between two areas (used if the
 * original connection is found to be bad).  Won't
 * un-cluster them.
 */

nosave del_area_link(fromroom, fromdir) {
    int fromarea = dd_list[fromroom][0];
    if (fromarea < 0)
		return;	/* never loaded? */

    array aentry = area_list[fromarea];
    int x = 0;	/* want to act on all the x mod 3 == 1 pairs (aentry == toarea, fromroom, fromdir) */
    int ns = 0;
    while (x >= 0) {
	x = index(aentry, fromroom, x+1);
	if ((x % 3) == 1) {
            TG("Deleting area link " + fromarea + ": " + seen_list[fromroom] + "; " + aentry[x+1])
	    aentry = aentry[..x-2] + aentry[x+2..];
	    ns=1;
	    x -= 1;
	    }
	}
    if (ns) {
	set_needs_save();
        area_list[fromarea] = aentry;
	}
    }

/*
 * Convert a room ID to an area ID.
 */

representative_room(int areaID) {
	if (areaID < 0)
		return (-1);
	array a = area_list[areaID];
	if (!a || !arrayp(a))
		return (-1);
	if (sizeof(a) < 3)
		return (-1);
	return a[1];	/* first from room */
	}

area_id(int roomNo) {
    if (arrayp(dd_list[roomNo]))
	    return dd_list[roomNo][0];
    return 0;
	}

/*
 * Given an area ID, find which cluster it is assigned to.
 */

scc_id(int area) {
	return find_component_base(scc_list, area);
	}

cluster_id(int area) {
	return find_component_base(cluster_list, area);
	}

/* Called when two areas have just had a link
 * installed (or discovered).  If neither is a controlled area,
 * link them into the same cluster.  Returns the no. of areas which
 * were controlled areas (0, 1 or 2).
 */

link_clusters(area1, area2) {
	int c1 = cluster_id(area1);
	int c2 = cluster_id(area2);

	if (c1 >= 0 && c2 >= 0 && c1 != c2) {
		nclusters = join_components(nclusters, cluster_list, c1, c2);
		/* To do: run through stripping dest dirs */
		return 0;
		}

	if (c1 < 0 && c2 < 0)
		return 2;

	return 1;
	}


link_sccs(area1, area2) {
	int c1 = scc_id(area1);
	int c2 = scc_id(area2);

	if (c1 >= 0 && c2 >= 0 && c1 != c2) {
		nsccs = join_components(nsccs, scc_list, c1, c2);
		/* To do: run through stripping dest dirs */
		return 0;
		}

	if (c1 < 0 && c2 < 0)
		return 2;

	return 1;
	}


/*
 * add_area_link(fromroom, toroom, dir) - used by the above.
 * Looks up the area that the room referenced by roomindex resides in and
 * adds a link from that area to 'area', specifying that you leave in
 * direction 'dir' from room 'roomindex'.  If a link already exists, and
 * it is not a door link (dirn contains a #), the link won't be replaced
 * with this one, otherwise it will.  If either other area is a controller,
 * we record all exits between them; this lets the MapKnower ask the
 * area controller(s) for the best exit to use (it also lets us
 * control routing between multiple controlled areas, which saves
 * wizzes implementing it in each controller).
 */

nosave add_area_link(fromroom, toroom, dir) {
	int r = add_area_link_u(area_list, fromroom, toroom, dir);
	if (r) {	/* new link */
		add_area_link_u(reverse_area_list, toroom, fromroom, dir);
		if (r > 0) {	/* between noncontrolled areas */
    		int fromarea = dd_list[fromroom][0];
    		int toarea = dd_list[toroom][0];
			link_clusters(fromarea, toarea);
			/* Quickie SCC check: see if toroom's area has a link back to
			 * fromroom's.  We collapse longer cycles elsewhere.
			 */
			if (scc_id(fromarea) != scc_id(toarea)) {
				int x;
				array aentry = area_list[toarea];
				for (x = 0; x < sizeof(aentry); x += 3) {
					if (area_id(aentry[x]) == fromarea) {
						link_sccs(fromarea, toarea);
						break;
						}
					}
				}
			}
		}
	}

/*
 * actually add a link.  Returns 0 if no change, -1 if one or both were
 * controlled, 1 if neither was controlled.
 */

nosave add_area_link_u(array alist, fromroom, toroom, dir) {
    int i;
    int fromarea = dd_list[fromroom][0];
    int toarea = dd_list[toroom][0];

    if (fromarea == toarea)
		return 0;

    array aentry = alist[fromarea];

	/* don't want to call cluster_id until we have made the link */
	int havecontroller = (cluster_list[fromarea] == (-1)) || (cluster_list[toarea] == (-1));
	

/* See whether we already have a link to our area.  Have to be careful -
 * area dest-dirs are `toroom, fromroom, dir'; area & fromroom are both
 * numbers.  If either or both areas are controlled, we record _all_ exits.
 */
	int found = 0;
    int i = 0;
    for (i = 0; i < sizeof(aentry); i += 3) {
		if (area_id(aentry[i]) != toarea)
			continue;
		if (!havecontroller) {
			found = 1;	/* found an exit to the same area - good enough */
			break;	/* only keep one link between non-controlled areas */
			}
		/* we have a controller - check whether we already have the exit */
		if (alist[i+1] == fromroom)
			return 0;
		}

    if (!found) {
	   	alist[fromarea] += ({ toroom, fromroom, dir });
	   	if (havecontroller)
			return -1;
		return 1;
	   	}

	/* we already had an exit to the area */
    if (index(dir, "#") >= 0)
		return 0;

/* update if old route required a key and new route doesn't */
    if (index(aentry[i+2], "#") >= 0) {
		alist[fromarea] = aentry[.. i-1]
			    + aentry[i+3..]
			    + ({ toroom, fromroom, dir });
		return 1;
	}
    return 0;
}

/*
 * Support for basic directional, area effects.
 * Given a room name, a distance, and a set of directions or
 * flood attributes, return all rooms that can be reached within
 * that distance & flood, to a max of MAX_AREA_EFFECT.
 * Gandalf, Jan 95.
 */

find_flood(string fromroom,
	   int dist,
	   (int|array|string) dirns,
	   (int|array) prev) {

    array build = allocate(MAX_AREA_EFFECT);
    int bstart = 0;
    int bend = 1;

    if (pointerp(prev)) {
	build = prev + build;
	bend = sizeof(prev);
	}

    int x;
    int h = hash_name(fromroom);

    int bdist = 0;
    int bdstart = bend;	/* here-bend is the outer room frontier */

    if (!seen_list[h])
	return 0;

    if (stringp(dirns))
	dirns = ({ dirns });

    build[bstart] = h;
    while (bstart < bend && bdist < dist) {
	h = build[bstart];
	array dd = dd_list[h][4];
	for (x = 0; (bdist < dist) &&
		    (x < sizeof(dd)) &&
		    (bend < MAX_AREA_EFFECT); x += 2) {
	    if (intp(dirns) || (index(dirns, strip_code(dd[x+1])) >= 0)) {
		if (index(build, dd[x]) < 0) {
		    build[bend++] = dd[x];
		    }
		}
	    }
	bstart++;
	if (bstart >= bdstart) {
	    bdstart = bend;
	    bdist++;
	    }
	}

    array realthing = allocate(bend);
    for (x = 0; x < bend; x++) {
	realthing[x] = seen_list[build[x]];
	}

    return realthing;
}



/*
 * This queue stuff is ripped from hunter's code - G.
 */

/*
 * Simple queue data structure management object, this time with
 * weights.
 *
 * Hunter of Shattered Worlds (hacked)
 */

nosave array Queue;
nosave array Weights;
nosave array Seen;

clear() { 
	Queue = ({ });
	Weights = ({ });
	Seen = ({ });
	return 1;
}

popw() {
array Ret;
	if ((!Queue) || nil()) return 0; 
	Ret = ({ Queue[0], Weights[0] });
	Seen += ({ Queue[0] });
	Queue = Queue[1..];
	Weights = Weights[1..];
	return Ret;
}

pop() {
int Ret;
	if ((!Queue) || nil()) return 0; 
	Ret = Queue[0];
	Seen += ({ Ret });
	Queue = Queue[1..];
	Weights = Weights[1..];
	return Ret;
}

push(arg, weight) {
	int pos = 0;
	if (!Queue) Queue = ({ });
	if (!Weights) Weights = ({ });
	if (weight >= 0)
	    while ((pos < sizeof(Weights))
	      && (Weights[pos] <= weight))
		  pos++;
	    else
		pos = sizeof(Weights);
	if (pos >= sizeof(Weights)) {
	    Queue += ({arg});
	    Weights += ({ weight });
	    }
	else {
	    Queue = Queue[..pos-1] + ({ arg }) + Queue[pos..];
	    Weights = Weights[..pos-1] + ({ weight }) + Weights[pos..];
	}
	return 1;
}

nil() { return ((!pointerp(Queue)) || (sizeof(Queue) == 0)); }

seen(x) { return ((index(Seen, x) != (-1)) || (index(Queue, x) != (-1))); }

/*
 * Cache stuff so that we don't have to recompute too often.  Cache indexes
 * are stored as strings; if they are an integer, the slot is free.
 * Each cache slot is a pair: a string, and an (array) path.  The first item
 * in a cache array is an array of 3 stats: the first free cache slot, the
 * number of queries, and the number of hits.
 */

init_caches() {
    if (!pointerp(inter_cache)) {
        inter_cache = allocate(1 + (INTER_AREA_CACHE * 2));
        inter_cache[0] = ({ 1, 1, 0 }); /* nextfree, reads, hits */
	}
    if (!pointerp(intra_cache)) {
        intra_cache = allocate(1 + (INTRA_AREA_CACHE * 2));
        intra_cache[0] = ({ 1, 1, 0 }); /* nextfree, reads, hits */
	}
    return 1;
}

/* check_cache - do an associative lookup, if found, move closer to head of list
 * and return path.  Cache elts: c[0] - stats.  c[x, x+1]: iden, path
 * Cache retention algorithm changed - G - We want to move a cache item 1 towards
 * the head of the cache per hit (asymptotically, this is optimal, but has a poor
 * startup time).  However, path accesses tend to be clustered; when the cache is
 * full, but a new path is added, we expect that the new path, or something similar,
 * will be accessed again in the near future (eg when 20 monsters decide to make a
 * run for the pub).  So, when swapping, we have a random chance of swapping with
 * the adjacent elt (80% of the time), or a random higher elt.
 */

check_cache(c, s) {
    int x;
    int swaploc;
    array pa;	/* for swap */
    string ps;

    if (!stringp(s))	/* should throw an error */
	return 0;

    c[0][1]++;	/* num queries */
    if ((x = index(c, s)) < 0)
	return 0;
    c[0][2]++;	/* num hits */

    pa = c[x+1];	/* found it: pa is path */

    if (!pointerp(pa)) return 0;	/* this won't allow recomputations if it's in cache and stats change */

    if (x >= 3) {			/* second or successive entry; swap adjacent elts so we */
	if (random(1000) > 200)		/* This is coz we always add new items to the end. */
		swaploc = x-2;
	else
		swaploc = random(x/2) * 2 + 1;
	c[x+1] = c[swaploc + 1];	/* eventually settle down to an optimal cache ordering */
	c[swaploc + 1] = pa;		/* would it be better to do a heap-like cache? */
	c[x] = c[swaploc];
	c[swaploc] = s;
	}

    return pa;
}

/* add_cache - adds an elt to cache, returns the cache. */
/* NB: cache size must be odd and >= 3 */

nosave add_cache(c, s, p) {
    int x;
    int prev;
    int next;

if (!arrayp(c)) return;
if (!sizeof(c)) return;
if (!sizeof(c[0])) return;

/* Find a cache entry */
    x = c[0][0];
    if (x >= sizeof(c)) { /* full cache - put this in as the last entry */
	x = sizeof(c) - 2;	/* cache size must be odd */
	}
    else
	c[0][0] += 2;

    c[x] = s;
    c[x+1] = p;
    return c;
}

cache_stats(c) {
    return "Entries: " + (c[0][0] / 2) + "/" + (sizeof(c)/2) +
			"	Hits: " + c[0][2] + "/" + c[0][1] +
			"		" + ((c[0][2] * 100) / c[0][1]) + "%";
    }

/*
 * rebuild the cluster list from scratch (for when the
 * clustering has been screwed by a (since-deleted) wiz:
 *
 * Make a new cluster array
 * Initialise it with a seperate cluster ID for each area, except:
 * areas currently listed as area (-1) are initialised to that in the
 * new array (these are controllers)
 * Running forward through the area list, use the area exits to
 * join clusters.
 * Assign the result to the cluster list.
 *
 * Written because the (not yet deleted) author screwed the original algorithm.
 */

rebuild_cluster_list() {
	array newlist = allocate(AREA_TABLE_SIZE);
	int x = 0;
	int nclust = next_area;
	while (x < next_area) {
		if (cluster_list[x] < 0) {
			newlist[x] = (-1);
			nclust--;
			}
		else {
			newlist[x] = x;
			}
		x++;
		}
	write("cl: 1 \n");
	/* cluster the current areas */
	x = 0;
	while (x < next_area) {
		int y = 0;
		array aentry = area_list[x];
		if (aentry && (newlist[x] >= 0)) {
			/* get our area's initial cluster ID */
			int clus = find_component_base(newlist, x);
			while (y < sizeof(aentry)) {
				/* aentry - toroom, fromroom, fromdir */
				int aid = area_id(aentry[y]);
				if (aid >= 0 && newlist[aid] >= 0)
					nclust = join_components(nclust, newlist, x, area_id(aentry[y]));
				y += 3;
				}
			}
		x++;
		}
	write("cl: 2; nclust = " + nclust + " \n");
	/* resolve chains */
	int cnum = 0;
	x = 0;
	while (x < next_area) {
		if (newlist[x] >= 0) {
			if (newlist[x] < x) {
				newlist[x] = newlist[newlist[x]];
				}
			else {
				cnum++;
				}
			}
		x++;
		}
	write("cl: 3; cnum = " + cnum + " \n");
	cluster_list = newlist;
	nclusters = cnum;
	}

/*
 * Traverse an area graph, taking many parameters and returning
 * many results :)
 *
 * Arguments:
 *		1. The area graph (probably area_list)
 *		2. The room to start from.
 *		3. A terminator list (cluster ID map or SCC map) 
 *		4. Backtrack_ids, filled in with (PrevArea + 1)
 *		5. Numbers, areas are sequentially numbered when seen
 *		6. First number to use, must be >= 1.
 *		7. Seen_sequence, has areas IDs added in the order seen
 *		8. First_seen - the first slot to use in seen_sequence.
 *		9. From_move - the source room in each exit traversed
 *		10. To_move - the target room in each exit traversed
 *		11. dir_move - the direction of each exit traversed
 *		12. move_count - first slot to use in the above 3 arrays
 *		12. Flags, described below.
 *
 * The area graph and start area must be supplied.  Of the rest,
 * any array can be null, and will not be filled in.  If there
 * is a terminator list, it will be used to restrict the search
 * to the areas having the same IDs as the start area.  The
 * backtrack IDs and numbers arrays are marked with the previous
 * area seen and sequential ordering for each area; these arrays
 * are also used for loop detection, and a search will terminate
 * at any room where either quantity is nonzero (if neither of
 * these are supplied, or if we aren't marking enough stuff in them,
 * an internal array will be created to control backtracking).
 *
 * The return value is a 3-element array: the final values of
 * the number, first_seen, and move_count integers.  Even if we
 * are not actually recording these values in arrays (because no
 * arrays were supplied), the quantities will still be incremented
 * and returned.
 */

#define F_log_interior 	(1)		/* put interior nodes in seen_list */
#define F_log_leaves 	(2)		/* put leav nodes in seen_list */
#define F_log_external 	(4)		/* put external nodes in seen_list */

#define F_mark_interior (16)	/* mark all interior (non-leaf) nodes */
#define F_mark_leaves 	(32)	/* mark leaves (numbering or backtrack) */
#define F_mark_external (64)	/* mark reached external nodes */

#define F_exits_internal (128)	/* log exits to internal nodes */
#define F_exits_external (256)	/* log exits to external nodes */
#define F_exits_first_only (8)		/* not implemented */

find_reachable(area_list,		/* graph to traverse */
			   terminators,		/* clusters/SCCs */
			   x,				/* start area */
			   backtrack_ids,	/* prev area IDs */
			   numbers,			/* order seen */
			   nextid,			/* first ID */
			   seen_sequence,	/* seen sequence (area output queue) */
			   seen_count,		/* where to start in seen array */
			   from_move,		/* seen sequence for moves */
			   to_move,			/* seen sequence */
			   dir_move,		/* seen sequence */
			   move_count,		/* first free slot in move array */
			   flags) {			/* 1: all interior areas. 2: breadth */

	/* Flags:
	 *	log_xxx - log traversed areas in seen_sequence.
	 *	mark_xxx - mark areas in backtrack_id or numbers
	 *	exits_xxx - log exits from internal areas to other areas.
	 */

	int log_interior = 0 != (flags & F_log_interior );	/* all interior = fill (else only log moves that leave the cluster) */
	int log_leaves = 0 != (flags & F_log_leaves);
	int log_external = 0 != (flags & F_log_external);

	int mark_interior = 0 != (flags & F_mark_interior);	/* all interior = fill (else only log moves that leave the cluster) */
	int mark_leaves = 0 != (flags & F_mark_leaves);
	int mark_external = 0 != (flags & F_mark_external);

	int exits_internal = 0 != (flags & F_exits_internal);
	int exits_external = 0 != (flags & F_exits_external);

	int have_numbers = arrayp(numbers);
	int have_terminators = arrayp(terminators);
	int have_backtrack = arrayp(backtrack_ids);
	int have_seen = arrayp(seen_sequence);
	int have_from = arrayp(from_move);
	int have_to = arrayp(to_move);
	int have_dir = arrayp(dir_move);

	int have_exits = (have_from || have_to || have_dir)
				  && (exits_internal || exits_external);

	/* we need to ensure we can detect loops and previously processed rooms.
	 * We never push external areas on the stack, so it is sufficient to
	 * ensure we can record all internal nodes.
	 */

	int have_internal = 0;
	array internal;

	if (!mark_interior || !mark_leaves) {	/* not logging everything */
		have_internal = 1;
		}

	if (log_external && !mark_external) {	/* externals will be pushed */
		have_internal = 1;
		}

	if (!have_numbers && !have_backtrack) {	/* nowhere to log it! */
		have_internal = 1;
		}

	if (have_internal) {
		internal = allocate(next_area);
		}

	int num_added = 0;

	array area_stack = allocate(next_area);
	array previd_stack = allocate(next_area);

	int stacktop = 0;

	int term;
	if (have_terminators) {
		if (x >= 0)
			term = find_component_base(terminators, area_id(x));
		}

	area_stack[stacktop] = x;
	previd_stack[stacktop++] = x;

	while (stacktop > 0) {
		int included = (-1);
		int push = 1;
		int room_id = area_stack[--stacktop];
		int area = area_id(room_id);
		int prev_room = previd_stack[stacktop];

		if (area < 0)
			continue;

		/* check whether the area was already reached */

		int done1 = (have_numbers && numbers[area]);
		int done2 = (have_backtrack && backtrack_ids[area]);
		int done3 = (have_internal && internal[area]);

		if (done1 || done2 || done3)
			continue;

		/*
		 * For any node we see, the internal list must be updated.
		 * For other lists, we must work out the node type first,
		 * which implies processing all of the exits (unless
		 * it is external).
		 */

		if (have_internal)
			internal[area] = 1;

		/*
		 * If this is an external node, we need not process exits.
		 */

		int do_exits = 1;
		int mark_node = mark_interior;
		int log_node = log_interior;
		int is_leaf = 0;

		if (have_terminators) {
			int t = find_component_base(terminators, area);
			if (t != term) {
				do_exits = 0;
				mark_node = mark_external;
				log_node = log_external;
				}
			}

		/*
		 * Check exits to find out what we are.
		 * While we're here, log exits and push destinations.
		 */

        /* internal or leaf */
	    array exits = area_list[area];
		if (do_exits && arrayp(exits)) 
        {	
			int i;
			for (i = 0; i < sizeof(exits); i += 3) {
				/* if we are processing the reverse graph, either the
				 * exit or the entrance could be in our area - check both.
				 */
				int log_exit = 0;
				int Tr = exits[i];
				int Fr = exits[i+1];
				int Ta = area_id(Tr);
				if (Ta == area) {
					Fr = Tr;
					Tr = (exits[i+1]);
					Ta = area_id(Tr);
					}
				if (Ta == area) { /* this is really an error */
					throw("Exit from area " + Ta + " to itself.");
					continue;
					}

				/*
				 * Check whether the target area was already reached.
				 * If it was, we won't push or log it again.
				 */

				int target_reached = 0;

				if (Ta >= 0) {
					target_reached += (have_numbers && numbers[Ta]);
					target_reached += (have_backtrack && backtrack_ids[Ta]);
					target_reached += (have_internal && internal[Ta]);
					}
				else {			/* don't process exits to unloaded rooms */
					continue;
					}

				int tc = term;
				if (have_terminators)
					tc = find_component_base(terminators, Ta);

				if (tc != term) {	/* external exit; we are a leaf */
					mark_node = mark_leaves;
					log_node = log_leaves;
					is_leaf = 1;
					if (exits_external)
						log_exit = 1;
					if (!mark_external && !log_external)
						target_reached = 1;
					}
				else {
					if (exits_internal)
						log_exit = 1;
					}
				if (log_exit) {
					if (have_from)
						from_move[move_count] = Fr;
					if (have_to)
						to_move[move_count] = Tr;
					if (have_dir)
						dir_move[move_count] = exits[i + 2];
					move_count++;
					}
				if (!target_reached) {
					/* we will push controlled areas multiple times.  Ho hum */
					area_stack[stacktop] = Tr;
					previd_stack[stacktop++] = Fr;
					}
				}
			}
	
		if (mark_node) {
			if (have_numbers)
				numbers[area] = nextid;
			nextid++;
			if (have_backtrack)
				backtrack_ids[area] = prev_room + 1;
			}
		
		if (log_node) {
			if (have_seen)
				seen_sequence[seen_count] = area;
			seen_count++;
			}
		}

	return ({ nextid, seen_count, move_count });
	}

/*
 * Calculate strongly connected components.
 */

rebuild_scc_list() {
	array newlist = allocate(AREA_TABLE_SIZE);
	int x = 0;
	int y;
	int nscc = next_area;
	while (x < next_area) {
		if (scc_list[x] < 0) {
			newlist[x] = (-1);
			nscc--;
			}
		else {
			newlist[x] = x;
			}
		x++;
		}
	write("sl: 1 \n");

	/*
	 * scc the current areas.
	 */

	array numbers = allocate(AREA_TABLE_SIZE);
	int nextid = 1;
	x = (-1);
	while (1) {
		x = index(numbers, 0, x+1);	/* find first untouched node */
		if (x < 0)
			break;
		if (x >= next_area)
			break;	/* numbered them all */
		if (newlist[x] < 0)
			continue;	/* controller */

		array res = find_reachable(area_list,		/* graph to traverse */
			   cluster_list,	/* clusters/SCCs */
			   representative_room(x),				/* start area */
			   0,				/* prev area IDs */
			   numbers,			/* order seen */
			   nextid,			/* first ID */
			   0,				/* seen sequence (area output queue) */
			   0,				/* where to start in seen array */
			   0,				/* seen sequence for moves */
			   0,				/* seen sequence */
			   0,				/* seen sequence */
			   0,				/* first free slot in move array */
			 F_mark_interior | F_mark_leaves);				/* flags: */

		nextid = res[0];
		}

	array seen_list = allocate(AREA_TABLE_SIZE);
	array othernumbers = allocate(AREA_TABLE_SIZE);	/* so it doesn't continually alloc it own backtrack array */

	for (x = 0; x < next_area; x++) {
		int i = index(numbers, x+1);	/* find next id */
		if (i < 0)						/* already in SCC */
			continue;
		if (newlist[i] != i)	/* in SCC */
			continue;
		res = find_reachable(reverse_area_list,	/* graph to traverse */
			   cluster_list,	/* clusters/SCCs */
			   representative_room(i),				/* start area */
			   0,				/* prev area IDs */
			   othernumbers,		/* order seen */
			   1,				/* first ID */
			   seen_list,		/* seen sequence (area output queue) */
			   0,				/* where to start in seen array */
			   0,				/* seen sequence for moves */
			   0,				/* seen sequence */
			   0,				/* seen sequence */
			   0,				/* first free slot in move array */
			 F_mark_interior | F_mark_leaves | F_log_interior | F_log_leaves );

		int nareas = res[1];
		if (nareas != res[0] - 1)
			write("Bad counts: " + nareas + ", " + res[0]);
		for (y = 0; y < nareas; y++) {
			nscc = join_components(nscc, newlist, i, seen_list[y]);
			}
		}
		
	write("cl: 2; nscc = " + nscc + " \n");
	/* resolve chains */
	int cnum = 0;
	x = 0;
	while (x < next_area) {
		if (newlist[x] >= 0) {
			if (newlist[x] < x) {
				newlist[x] = newlist[newlist[x]];
				}
			else {
				cnum++;
				}
			}
		x++;
		}
	write("cl: 3; cnum = " + cnum + " \n");
	scc_list = newlist;
	nsccs = cnum;
	}

/*
 * Rebuild area exits - for times when you've had to un-fuck the map.
 * Expensive.
 */

rebuild_area_exits() {
	}

rebuild_area_entrances() {
	}

/*
 * Given an array (eg cluster list) and two components
 * within it (to be joined), join them.
 */

find_component_base(array a, int x) {
	if (a[x] < 0)
		return a[x];
	
	int top = x;
	while (a[x] != x)
		x = a[x];

	while (top != x) {
		int y = a[top];
		a[top] = x;
		top = y;
		}
	
	return x;
	}

int join_components(int ncomps, array a, int x1, int x2) {
	int c1 = find_component_base(a, x1);
	int c2 = find_component_base(a, x2);
	if (c1 != c2)
		ncomps--;
	if (c1 < c2)
		a[c2] = c1;
	else
		a[c1] = c2;
	return ncomps;
	}

/*
 * For map builder.
 */

get_controllers() {
	return controller_list;
	}

get_seen_list() {
	return seen_list;
	}

/*
 * The following stuff is for calculation of routes through the
 * graph.  It is done as a multistep process - after a monster calls
 * this, giving FROM, TO, and a call back routine, it should compute a
 * partial route between FROM and TO, and call the monster's callback
 * routine, giving it an array of rooms to get to and the direction
 * to leave in; if this is zero, the MapKnower may not know how to get
 * to the place.  An empty array means FROM and TO were the same place.
 * The first room in the list may be the FROM room, in which case the monster
 * should simply make the associated move; if not, the monster should
 * re-call this object asking directions from his room to the first room
 * in the list.
 * A monster moves as follows:
 *    Call this object with FROM, TO, and callback rtne.
 *    when the callback happens:
 *	If the given array is 0, there is no route (yet)
 *	If the array is ({ }), FROM = TO
 *    Otherwise, for each element in the array:
 *	Check whether we are in the designated room.
 *	    If so, move in the indicated direction (using command()).
 *	    If not, ask the server for a route to the named room, follow it,
 *		and go back to using the old route.
 *
 * This routine returns one of the following:
 *     -1 - Bad room name(s)
 *	0 - search started; await callback.
 *	1 - callback ob or callback routine nonexistent
 *	2 - FROM never seen - will not call callback (even with 0).
 *	3 - TO never seen - will not call callback
 *
 * The second param is given as an extra parameter on the callback (it need
 * not be used) - for accounting purposes for the object (it's really hard to
 * manage your callbacks when another object has the callouts and you can't
 * even tell whether it's got one scheduled for you or not).  It was actually
 * added when I decided that "track.lpc" needed to be able to change its
 * mind about what route it was taking; in this case, param is a sequence
 * number which is incremented by track (see std/map/track.c).
 */

find_route(fromroom, to, ob, callback, param) {
    int Fh, Th;
    int a1 = (-1), a2 = (-1);

    if (!objectp(ob) || !stringp(callback))
	// || !function_exists(callback, ob))
	    return 1;
    
/* make sure that we can get to interior rooms of a controlled
 * area (that we may not have seen yet)
 */

    string c1 = controller_name(fromroom);
    string c2 = controller_name(to);
    if (c1 && c1 == c2) {
	    /* same (controlled) area - strip args and pass them in */
	    return c1->find_route(fromroom, to, ob, callback, param);
	}

    if (c1)
		a1 = controller_area(c1);
    else {
        Fh = hash_name(fromroom);
        if (!seen_list[Fh] || (catch { forced_load(Fh); })) {
	    /* room has never been seen; loading it wont */
	    	return (2);	  /* add any usable links */
	    	}
        if ((a1 = dd_list[Fh][0]) < 0) {
	    	return(2);
	    	}
		}

    if (c2)
	a2 = controller_area(c2);
    else {
        Th = hash_name(to);
        if (!seen_list[Th] || (catch { forced_load(Th); })) {
	    return (3);
	    }
        if ((a2 = dd_list[Th][0]) < 0) {
	    return(3);
	    }
	}

	/*
	 * We have rooms, their areas (which are real areas).
	 * Choose a search algorithm.
	 */

    if (a1 == a2)
		return intra_area_search(Fh, Th, ob, callback, param);
	
	return inter_area_search(Fh, Th, ob, callback, param);
}

forced_load(HashNum) {
    if (dd_list[HashNum][0] != (-1)) /* already in an area */
	return;
    seen_list[HashNum] -> short();
    }

/* inter area search - given (from area, to area, ob, callback) return a list
 * of rooms to get to and directions to go.  Routes are cached whenever the from
 * and to rooms are not controlled (because of the way we calculate this,
 * routes that traverse controlled areas will cache the route to the exit
 * to the controlled area, then from the exit of the controlled area to the
 * destination room; the controllers are responsible for choosing routes
 * within their own area).
 *
 * The algorithm is as follows:
 *	If source and destination are within the same SCC, calculate a direct route,
 *	without leaving the SCC.  These are cached as inter-area routes.
 *
 *	Otherwise, *attempt* to get a route which remains within the cluster.
 *	If this works, use it.
 *
 *	Otherwise, we have a list of plausible cluster exits to use.  Use the
 *	reverse area list to build a list of plausible cluster entrances that
 *	will reach the target room.  If any exits and entrances are to/from the
 *	same controller, we will route through it: use the controller to find
 *	the closest exit/entrance pair (if it doesn't work, choose a pair
 *	arbitrarily), save the route from the start room to the controlled area
 *	and from the exit of the controlled area to the end room, and allow
 *	the router object to call back to get the route through the controlled
 *	area.
 *
 *	Otherwise, we need to traverse multiple controlled areas.  This
 *	is rare (and expensive).  Calculate a simgle, complete, inter-area
 *	route.  Run through the area list picking out the sequence of
 *	controlled areas to traverse.
 */

inter_area_search(Fh, Th, ob, callback, param) {
	int Fa = area_id(Fh);
	int Ta = area_id(Th);
    array path;
    int ca;
    int x;
    array prevarea;
	int usecluster = (-1);
	if (cluster_id(Fa) == cluster_id(Ta))
		usecluster = cluster_id(Fa);
	usecluster = cluster_id(Ta);

	/*
	 * If both rooms are in valid clusters, any cached route will be valid.
	 * Otherwise, the correct exit (or entrance) to use to a controlled
	 * area depends on the location of the room therein.
	 */
	if (cluster_id(Fa) >= 0 && cluster_id(Ta) >= 0) {
    	if (x = check_cache(inter_cache, Fa + "." + Ta)) {
	    	do_call_other(ob, callback, x, param);
	    	return 0;
			}
		}

	/*
	 * If the rooms are in the same SCC, we are guaranteed a reasonable
	 * path within the cluster.  If they are in the same cluster, we may
	 * or may not find a route within the cluster (but it's the first
	 * step in computing exits, so...)
	 */

    path = ({ });
    area_backtrack = allocate(sizeof(area_list));	/* initialise to 0, not that we have to here */
    clear();
    area_backtrack[Fa] = (-1); /* no area to backtrack to */
    push(Fa, (-1));
    while (1) {
	if (nil()) { /* didn't find it */
	    do_call_other(ob, callback, 0, param);	/* clear backtrack? */
	    clear();
	    return 0;
	    }
	ca = pop();
	if (ca == Ta) {	/* found it - backtrack */
	    path = ({ });
	    while (ca != Fa) {
		int pa = area_backtrack[ca];
		prevarea = area_list[pa];
		area_backtrack[ca] = 0;
		x = 0;
		while (x < sizeof(prevarea) && area_id(prevarea[x]) != ca) {
			x += 3;
			}
		if (x >= sizeof(prevarea))
			throw("Internal error in find_route (backtrack)");
		path = ({ seen_list[prevarea[x+1]], prevarea[x+2] }) + path;
		ca = pa;
		}
	    inter_cache = add_cache(inter_cache, Fa + "." + Ta, path);
	    do_call_other(ob, callback, path, param);
	    clear();
	    return 0;
	    }
	for (x = 0; x < sizeof(area_list[ca]); x += 3) {
		int id = area_id(area_list[ca][x]);
	    if (!seen(id)) {
			if ((usecluster < 0) || (usecluster == cluster_id(id))) {
				push(id, -1);
				area_backtrack[id] = ca;
				}
		}
	    }
	}
}

/*
 * intra_area_search(fromroom, toroom, ob, callback) - both "rooms" here
 * are hash numbers.  Use a heuristic on the x/y coords of the two
 * rooms (which are guaranteed to be in the same area).
 */

#define abs(x)	(((x)>=0)? (x) : (-(x)))
#define AbsTo(x, y) (abs(tx - (x)) + abs(ty - (y)))

intra_area_search(fh, th, ob, callback, param) {
    array path;
    int tmp;
    int ca;
    int x;
    int tx;
    int ty;
    array prevdd;

    int pushcnt = 0, tstcnt = 0;

    if (fh == th) {
	    do_call_other(ob, callback, ({}), param);
	    return 0;
	}

    if (x = check_cache(intra_cache, fh + "#" + th)) {
	    do_call_other(ob, callback, x, param);
	    return 0;
	}

    room_backtrack = allocate(sizeof(seen_list));	/* initialise to 0, to support a fast homing heuristic */

    int myarea = dd_list[th][0];

    tx = dd_list[th][1];
    ty = dd_list[th][2];
    path = ({ });
    clear();
    room_backtrack[fh] = (-1); /* no room to backtrack to */
    push(fh, AbsTo(dd_list[fh][1], dd_list[fh][2]));
    while (1) {
	if (nil()) { /* didn't find it */
	    do_call_other(ob, callback, 0, param);
	    clear();
	    return 0;
	    }
	ca = (pop());
	tstcnt++;
	if (ca == th) {	/* found it - backtrack */
	    path = ({ });
	    do {
		tmp = ca;
	        ca = room_backtrack[ca] - 1;	/* 0 entries were unused */
		if (ca < 0)
		    break;
		prevdd = dd_list[ca];
		x = index(prevdd[4], tmp);
		if (x < 0) {
		    throw("Internal error in find_route (intra_area)\n");
		    }
		path = ({ seen_list[ca], prevdd[4][x+1] }) + path;
		}
		   while (ca != fh); /* do */
	    intra_cache = add_cache(intra_cache, fh + "#" + th, path);
	    do_call_other(ob, callback, path, param);
	    clear();
	    return 0;
	    }
	/* ca != th -> need to search further.  If no dd, discard */
	if (pointerp(dd_list[ca][4])) {
	    for (x = 0; x < sizeof(dd_list[ca][4]); x += 2) {
	        tmp = dd_list[ca][4][x];
	        if ((!seen(tmp)) && (dd_list[tmp][0] == myarea)) {
		    push(tmp, AbsTo(dd_list[tmp][1], dd_list[tmp][2]));
		    room_backtrack[tmp] = ca+1;
		    pushcnt++;
		    }
	        }
	    }
	else {
	/*
	 * If we got here, a room which was reached in an intra-area search had no dest dir listed.
	 * Apparently when I wrote the code, I was under the impression that this should never happen.
	 * But now, some years down the track, it appears to (for a very particular set of rooms - depends
	 * on the order they are loaded).  Anyone know why? - G.
	 * UPDATE: I remember finding the bug, kicking myself and fixing it.  But I cant remember what it was - G :)
	 *
	 * Addendum: room/city/pr1 doesn't appear to have a dest dir with the MapKnower, when
	 * the map is generated from a clean mud (the city guards get caught there).
	 */
	    TG("Mapknower: dest dir fuckup detected for " +
			seen_list[ca] + " looking for " + seen_list[th] + "\n")
	    }
	}
}

do_call_other(ob, callback, path, param) {
    call_out("do_callback", 1, ({ ob, callback, path, param }));
    }

do_callback(a) {
    if (objectp(a[0]))
    call_other(a[0], a[1], a[2], a[3]);
    }

/*
 * Tests, check functions, etc.
 */

show_room(w) {
    int h;
    int x;
    h = hash_name(w);
    (int|string) co = controller_name(w);
    if (co)
	write("Area controller: " + co + "; assigned area " + controller_area(co) + "\n");
    else
	write(w + " has no assigned area controller.\n");
    if (!(seen_list[h])) {
	write(w + " not known (as a room) by MapKnower\n");
	return;
	}
    write(w + " : hashes to " + h + "; area " + dd_list[h][0] + " (" + dd_list[h][1] + ", " + dd_list[h][2] + ")\n");
    if (dd_list[h][0] < 0) {
	write ("not loaded; seen in the following places:\n");
	}
    else
		write("Dest-dir list:\n");

    if (pointerp(dd_list[h][4])) {
        for (x = 0; x < sizeof(dd_list[h][4]); x += 2) {
	    write(seen_list[dd_list[h][4][x]] + " [" + dd_list[h][4][x] + "] : " + dd_list[h][4][x+1] + "\n");
	    }
	}
    else
	write("DEST DIR LIST NOT INITIALISED\n");
    show_area(dd_list[h][0]);
}

show_area(a, rev) {
    write("Area stats: area " + a + " has cluster ID " + cluster_id(a) + "; dd list:\n");
	show_area_dds(area_list, a);
	if (rev) {
		write("REVERSE dd list:\n");
		show_area_dds(reverse_area_list, a);
		}
	return 1;
	}

count_groups(array ltype, int nelts, array a) {
	int x;
	int c = 0;
	for (x = 0; x < nelts; x++) {
		int r = ltype[x];
		if (r >= 0)
			a[r]++;
		if (r == x)
			c++;
		}
	return c;
	}

show_clusters() {
	array a = allocate(next_area);
	write("Areas in each cluster ("
			+ count_groups(cluster_list, next_area, a) + ")\n");
	int x;
	for (x = 0; x < next_area; x++) {
		if (a[x] < 0)
			write(x + ": controller ");
		else
			if (a[x] > 0)
				write(x + ": " + a[x] + " ");
		}
	write("\n");
	}

show_sccs() {
	array a = allocate(next_area);
	write("Areas in each SCC (" + count_groups(scc_list, next_area, a) + ")\n");
	int x;
	for (x = 0; x < next_area; x++) {
		if (a[x] < 0)
			write(x + ": controller ");
		else
			if (a[x] > 0)
				write(x + ": " + a[x] + " ");
		}
	write("\n");
	}

/*
 * This one shows all SCCs that are mapped into the same cluster.
 * These clusters are `suspicious', in that they must contain rooms
 * that you can't get to from within the cluster.
 */

show_scc_clusters() {
	array cc = allocate(next_area);
	array sc = allocate(next_area);
	write("Clusters: " + count_groups(cluster_list, next_area, cc) + "\n");
	write("Sccs: " + count_groups(scc_list, next_area, sc) + "\n");
	write("Clusters with non-matching SCC counts:\n");
	int x, y;
	for (x = 0; x < next_area; x++) {
		if (cluster_list[x] < 0)
			continue;
		if (scc_list[x] < 0) {
			write("SCC " + x + " negative!\n");
			continue;
			}
		int cl = cluster_list[x];
		if (x != cl)
			continue;

		if (scc_list[x] == cl) {
			if (cc[cl] == sc[cl]) {
				continue;
				}
			}

		/* bad */
		write("Cluster " + cl + "(" + cc[cl] + " areas) has SCCs ");
		for (y = x; y < next_area; y++) {
			if (cl != cluster_list[y])
				continue;
			/* only print it for new sccs, not every area */
			if (scc_list[y] != y)
				continue;
			write(scc_list[y] + "(" + sc[y] + ") ");
			/* if it has no exits or no entries, mark it */
			if (sc[y] > 1)
				continue;
			if (sizeof(area_list[y]) < 1)
				write("OUT ");
			if (sizeof(reverse_area_list[y]) < 1)
				write("IN ");
			}
		write("\n");
		}
	}

/*
 * Show all SCC exits for which the target room does not have a back-exit.
 * (Despite being in different SCCs, the target room could have a back
 * exit if it is part of a controlled area).
 * Useful for checking for map anomalies.
 */

show_unidirectional() {
	int x, y;
	for (x = 0; x < sizeof(seen_list); x++) {
		if (!seen_list[x])
			continue;
		if (dd_list[x][0] < 0)
			continue;
		int Fs = scc_id(area_id(x));
		array exits = dd_list[x][4];
		for (y = 0; y < sizeof(exits); y+=2) {
			int Tr = exits[y];
			if (dd_list[Tr][0] < 0)	/* not mapped */
				continue;
			int Ts = scc_id(area_id(Tr));
			if (Ts == Fs)
				continue;
			array backlinks = dd_list[Tr][4];
			int z;
			int found = 0;
			for (z = 0; z < sizeof(backlinks); z+= 2) {
				if (backlinks[z] == x) {
					found = 1;
					break;
					}
				}
			if (!found) {
#if 0
				write("Unidirectional link: " + room_id_desc(x)
					/* + " -> " + room_id_desc(Tr) */
					+ "\n");
				break;
#else
				write("Unidirectional link: " + room_id_desc(x)
					+ " -> " + room_id_desc(Tr) 
					+ "\n");
#endif
				}
			}
		}
	}


room_id_desc(int h) {
	int area = area_id(h);
	int scc = scc_id(area);
	int clus = cluster_id(area);
	return seen_list[h] + " [" + h + "," + area + "," + scc + "," + clus + "] ";
	}

show_area_dds(area_list, a) {
    int x;
    for (x = 0; x < sizeof(area_list[a]); x += 3) {
		write(area_id(area_list[a][x]) + " : "
			+ room_id_desc(area_list[a][x+1])
			+ " -> " + area_list[a][x+2] + " -> "
			+ room_id_desc(area_list[a][x]) + "\n"
			);
		}
    }


show_incoming(string room1, string room2) {
	int fh = hash_name(room1);
	int fa = area_id(fh);
	int th = hash_name(room2);
	int ta = area_id(th);

	array fn = allocate(next_area);
	array tn = allocate(next_area);

	array res = find_reachable(reverse_area_list,		/* graph to traverse */
			   cluster_list,	/* clusters/SCCs */
			   fh,				/* start area */
			   0,				/* prev area IDs */
			   fn,				/* order seen */
			   1,				/* first ID */
			   0,				/* seen sequence (area output queue) */
			   0,				/* where to start in seen array */
			   0,				/* seen sequence for moves */
			   0,				/* seen sequence */
			   0,				/* seen sequence */
			   0,				/* first free slot in move array */
			 F_mark_interior | F_mark_leaves);				/* flags: */

	res = find_reachable(reverse_area_list,		/* graph to traverse */
			   cluster_list,	/* clusters/SCCs */
			   th,				/* start area */
			   0,				/* prev area IDs */
			   tn,				/* order seen */
			   1,				/* first ID */
			   0,				/* seen sequence (area output queue) */
			   0,				/* where to start in seen array */
			   0,				/* seen sequence for moves */
			   0,				/* seen sequence */
			   0,				/* seen sequence */
			   0,				/* first free slot in move array */
			 F_mark_interior | F_mark_leaves);				/* flags: */

	write("Areas from which " + room1 + " and " + room2 + " are reachable: ");
	int x;
	for (x = 0; x < next_area; x++) {
		if (!fn[x] || !tn[x])	/* area couldn't reach both destinations */
			continue;
		write(x + " ");
		}
	write("\n");
	}


